# TPM Intake Assistant — Technical Notes

I wrote this document to explain how I built the TPM Intake Assistant,
how the different pages work, and how I made the assessment and
decision-support logic work.

The application is deliberately small. I built it as a single HTML file
using HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript. I did not use a frontend
framework, backend, database, paid service, or external API. My aim was
to keep it easy to run, easy to understand, and easy to change.

The main application file in the repository is `index.html`.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 1. My technical approach

I decided to keep the application as a single-page browser application.

The page has three main parts:

- The HTML provides the page structure and the main application
  containers.
- The CSS controls the layout, colors, responsive behavior, and print
  view.
- The JavaScript holds the intake questions, application state,
  navigation, checks, assessment rules, decision-support rules, and
  report generation.

I wrapped the JavaScript in an immediately invoked function and enabled
strict mode:

``` javascript
(function(){
  "use strict";

  // Application code
})();
```

This keeps the application code in its own scope instead of adding all
of its variables and functions to the browser’s global scope.

I chose this approach because the application does not need a server to
do its main job. The browser can collect the answers, evaluate them,
build the report, and create the downloadable files.

## 2. How I organized the questions

I wanted to avoid writing a separate form by hand for every page.
Instead, I defined the questions as data in one JavaScript array called
`STEPS`.

Each step has a title, an introduction, and a list of fields. Each field
has properties such as:

- `id` — the key I use to store and retrieve the answer.
- `name` — the shorter name used in assessment messages and reports.
- `label` — the question shown to the user.
- `type` — text, date, textarea, or select.
- `required` — whether the field is required for the intake.
- `hint` and `example` — optional help text.
- `checks` — the validation checks that apply to that answer.
- `options` — the available choices for a select field.

A simplified example looks like this:

``` javascript
{
  title: "Problem",
  intro: "Describe what is wrong or missing today.",
  fields: [
    {
      id: "problem",
      name: "Problem",
      label: "What problem are you trying to solve?",
      type: "textarea",
      required: true,
      checks: ["solution", "vague", "short"]
    }
  ]
}
```

I used field IDs as the link between the form, saved answers,
validation, assessment rules, and report. This means I can change a
question’s wording without changing the key used by the rest of the
application.

After defining `STEPS`, I build an `ALL` array containing every field
from every intake section. I use this flattened list when I need to
check the full request, rather than only the page currently on screen.

The first ten entries in `STEPS` are the intake pages. Assessment and
Report & Downloads are separate views generated by their own rendering
functions.

## 3. What happens on each page

### Page 1 — Request basics

I use this page to establish the basic identity of the request:

- Request title
- Requester and team
- Sponsor or approving executive
- Submission date
- One-sentence summary

The title, requester, sponsor, and summary are required. The date is
initialized to the current local date when there is no saved date.

I treat the sponsor as especially important because someone needs to own
the business outcome and make or secure the decisions required to move
the request forward.

### Page 2 — Problem

This page is about understanding the problem before discussing a
solution.

I ask what problem the requester is trying to solve, who is affected,
how the issue is handled today, and what evidence is available.

The problem statement and affected users are required. I added checks to
the problem statement because I want to catch answers that start with a
proposed solution or use wording that is too broad.

For example, an answer that starts with “We need to build…” may describe
a solution before explaining the problem. I flag it as a prompt to ask
what is wrong today and why it needs to change.

### Page 3 — Value and success

Here I capture the intended outcome and how the requester expects to
measure it.

The desired outcome and success metrics are required. I also ask about
the cost of doing nothing and the business or team goal connected to the
request.

The metric check looks for at least one number. It is a simple first
check, not a full review of whether the measure is useful. A person
still needs to confirm that the metric has a meaningful baseline,
target, and timeframe.

### Page 4 — Scope

I use this page to make the boundaries of the request clearer.

The requester describes what is in scope, what is out of scope, what
done should look like, and whether there is a smaller first phase.

Both the in-scope and out-of-scope fields are required. I included both
because a request can sound clear while still leaving important
boundaries open.

### Page 5 — People

This page captures the people and teams connected to the work.

I ask who will use the outcome, who must approve or be consulted, and
who will operate and support it after launch.

These fields are optional in the intake itself. I still use missing
answers in the decision-support logic because an initiative needs clear
decision-making and operational ownership before delivery commitments
are made.

### Page 6 — Requirements

I use this page to capture the main functional requirements,
integrations and data, and non-functional requirements.

I kept the questions open-ended because the level of technical detail
will vary between requests. The intake should capture what is known
without forcing every requester to provide a complete technical design.

### Page 7 — Security and compliance

This page records the highest data classification, whether personal or
regulated data is involved, whether a security review has been completed
or planned, and any compliance notes.

The data classification and security-review fields are required. I also
use these answers in a cross-field check. If the data is marked
Confidential or Restricted, or regulated data is involved, an empty,
unknown, or “Not needed” security-review answer is flagged for
confirmation.

The application does not decide whether a security review is formally
required. It highlights the combination of answers so the appropriate
security or privacy stakeholders can confirm the right approach.

### Page 8 — Technical context

I ask which systems and platforms are affected, what dependencies exist
on other teams or projects, and whether the current preference is to
build, buy, or leave that decision open.

The dependency answer is also used by the decision-support logic. If no
dependencies are recorded, I generate a prompt to confirm whether
dependencies exist or whether there are genuinely none.

### Page 9 — Timeline and resources

This page captures the target date, whether the date is hard or soft,
what is driving it, the budget or funding context, and the people or
teams already committed.

The target date and date driver are required. I added a cross-field
check for a hard deadline without a known date driver.

I also check whether a parseable target date is in the past. The date
field accepts free text so a requester can enter something such as “Q2
2027”. A quarter or other non-standard date may not be understood by the
browser’s date parser, so that check only works when the text can be
parsed as a date.

### Page 10 — Risks and rollout

I use this page to capture known risks, assumptions, constraints, and
rollout or adoption needs.

I also ask for priority context, including the impact of delay and how
the request compares with other work the requester knows about. This is
context for a discussion; it is not used to calculate a priority score.

### Page 11 — Assessment

The Assessment page is generated after the ten intake pages. It does not
collect a new set of answers. It reads the current answers, runs the
assessment and decision-support functions, and displays the findings.

I separated this page from the report page so I can review the
assessment first and then move to the finished report.

The page displays:

- Overall intake status
- The reason for that status
- Counts of blockers, important gaps, suggestions, and decisions to
  review
- The individual blockers, gaps, and suggestions
- Decisions and clarifications required
- Recommended next steps

Where an assessment finding is linked to an intake field, I include a
**Review answer** action. It takes me back to the relevant intake page.

The **Continue to Report & Downloads** button moves to Page 12.

### Page 12 — Report & Downloads

This page builds the full report from the current answers and calculated
findings.

I wanted this to be a proper output that I could share with a sponsor or
stakeholder, rather than just a screen showing the form answers.

The report contains:

- Executive summary
- Decision snapshot
- Business context
- TPM assessment
- Decisions and clarifications required
- Recommended next steps
- Decisions requiring attention
- Ownership gaps
- Dependencies to validate
- Stakeholder discussion questions
- Scope, outcomes, people, technical context, security, timeline, and
  delivery information
- Open questions
- Complete intake record

The page provides actions to return to Assessment, copy the report,
download Markdown, download a Word document, edit the answers, or print
the report.

The report is rebuilt from the current application state when the page
is rendered. I do not keep a separate copy of the report that could
become out of date when an answer changes.

## 4. How I manage application state and save answers

I keep the answers in a JavaScript object called `state`.

The main parts are:

- `state.answers` — the answers, stored by field ID.
- `state.step` — the current navigation index.
- `state.ack` — the validation warning signature acknowledged for each
  intake step during the current session.

I use this local storage key:

``` javascript
const KEY = "tpm-intake-v1";
```

When the application starts, it tries to read the saved data from
`localStorage`. If saved answers are found, I restore them and the last
page index. If there is no saved date, I use the current local date.

When a field changes, I update `state.answers` and call `save()`. The
`save()` function writes the answers and current step to `localStorage`.

I also save when the user moves between pages. This is why answers and
the current page remain available after a browser refresh in the same
browser.

I use `try/catch` around local storage access. If the browser blocks
storage or it is unavailable, the application can still run for the
current page session, although it cannot guarantee that answers will be
saved.

The **Start over** action asks for confirmation, resets the answers and
navigation to the beginning, and saves the reset state.

The `ack` validation signatures and the temporary `showEmpty` set are
held in memory. They are not written to local storage.

## 5. How I built the navigation and page rendering

I generate the left navigation from the ten entries in `STEPS`, then add
Assessment and Report & Downloads.

This gives me one navigation list for all 12 pages without manually
writing 12 separate navigation buttons.

I use the `go()` function to change the current step. It updates
`state.step`, saves the state, calls `render()`, scrolls to the top, and
moves keyboard focus to the main content.

The `render()` function clears the main content and decides which view
to display:

- If the current index is below the number of intake steps, it calls
  `renderStep()`.
- If the index is the Assessment position, it calls
  `renderAssessment()`.
- Otherwise, it calls `renderSummary()` for Report & Downloads.

For the intake pages, `fieldEl()` creates the correct input control
based on the field definition. It also adds the hint, optional example
where configured, the “Don’t know” action, the owner field, and the area
where validation messages appear.

This data-driven approach means I can add or change an intake question
mainly by editing the `STEPS` definition and then adding any specific
rule that the field needs.

## 6. How the validation checks work

I kept the validation logic in a small number of functions so I can
maintain it in one place.

### `fieldFlags()`

This function checks one field. It can check whether a required answer
is empty, whether an answer is marked Unknown, and whether any
configured checks apply to the answer.

The field’s `checks` array controls which text checks run.

### Vague wording check

I use a regular expression called `VAGUE` to look for words and phrases
such as:

- ASAP or urgent
- Soon
- Improve or better
- Faster or easier
- Scalable or robust
- Seamless or user-friendly
- Everyone, all users, or all teams
- Various or etc.

When one of these terms is found in a field that has the vague check
enabled, I show a message asking for a date, number, or example.

This is intentionally a simple pattern check. It does not understand the
full meaning of a sentence.

### Short-answer check

For fields with the `short` check, I flag an answer shorter than 25
characters. The aim is to prompt the requester to add context, not to
enforce a minimum writing length.

### Metric check

For fields with the `metric` check, I look for at least one digit. If no
number is present, I ask for a baseline and target.

This does not validate the quality of the metric or whether the target
is realistic.

### Solution-first check

I use the `SOLUTION` regular expression to check whether a problem
answer begins with wording such as “we need”, “build”, “create”,
“implement”, “deploy”, or “buy”.

If it does, I ask the requester to explain the problem the proposed
solution is meant to solve.

### Cross-field checks

The `crossFlags()` function looks at more than one answer at a time.

I currently use it for three checks:

1.  If the deadline type is Hard but the date driver is empty or
    Unknown, I ask what sets the deadline.
2.  If the target date can be parsed as a date and it is in the past, I
    ask the requester to check it.
3.  If the data classification is Confidential or Restricted, or
    regulated data is marked Yes, an empty, Unknown, or Not needed
    security-review answer is flagged for confirmation.

### What happens when someone clicks Next

The `next()` function runs the checks for the current intake page,
including empty required fields.

If it finds flags that have not yet been acknowledged for the current
set of answers, it displays the messages and asks the user to review
them. The user can correct the answers or press Next again to continue.

I use a signature made from the field IDs and messages. If the flags
change after an answer is edited, the signature changes and the user is
asked to review the new set of flags.

I chose this behavior because I want the application to raise important
questions without making every warning a hard stop. The user can
continue after reviewing the messages.

## 7. How I decide the intake status

The main assessment logic is in `buildAssessment()`.

I do not calculate a weighted score. I use a small set of rules and
separate the findings into blockers, gaps, and suggestions.

### Step 1 — Check for blockers

I treat two fields as blockers:

- Problem
- Sponsor

If either is empty or marked Unknown, I add a blocker. If the user has
entered a person or team in the “Who can answer this?” field, I include
that information in the finding.

If there is at least one blocker, the status is **Not actionable**.

### Step 2 — Check required information and material gaps

For other required fields, an empty or Unknown answer becomes an
important gap.

I also maintain a set of material fields:

- Summary
- Problem
- Affected users
- Desired outcome
- Success metrics
- In-scope
- Out-of-scope
- Target date
- Date driver
- Data classification
- Security review

If a validation check raises an issue on one of these material fields, I
place it in important gaps. Issues on other fields are placed in
suggestions.

I remove duplicate findings and sort them in the same order as the
intake questions. This makes the assessment easier to follow.

### Step 3 — Set the status

The status is determined in this order:

| Condition                                         | Status              |
|---------------------------------------------------|---------------------|
| One or more blockers exist                        | Not actionable      |
| No blockers, but one or more important gaps exist | Needs clarification |
| No blockers or important gaps exist               | Ready for Triage    |

I deliberately do not treat Ready for Triage as approval, funding,
prioritization, or permission to begin delivery. It only means the
intake has enough core information to start a triage discussion.

### Step 4 — Build general recommendations

The same function prepares a few general recommendations.

For example, it asks for a clearer problem and sponsor when those are
missing. If there are important gaps, it recommends assigning an owner
and target date to resolve them. If the success measure is missing,
Unknown, or has no number, it recommends defining a baseline, target,
and timeframe.

If none of those conditions apply, it recommends confirming the triage
participants and reviewing strategic fit, priority, capacity,
dependencies, and delivery options.

## 8. How I generate TPM Decision Support

I put the decision-support rules in a separate function called
`buildDecisionSupport()`. It receives the assessment result and checks
the answers again for specific ownership, decision, dependency,
security, and delivery questions.

I chose explicit rules instead of a score or an AI-generated
recommendation. I want to be able to trace each prompt back to an answer
in the intake.

### Decisions requiring attention

I create a decision item when certain information is missing or Unknown.

Examples include:

- Missing sponsor — identify the accountable business sponsor.
- Missing outcome or metrics — agree the intended outcome and how
  success will be measured.
- Missing scope boundaries — confirm what is included and excluded.
- Sensitive or regulated data without a confirmed security-review
  approach — confirm the required review.
- Missing deadline type or date driver — validate the target date and
  why it matters.

Each decision item includes a title, suggested decision owner,
information needed, and the reason the item was raised.

The suggested owner is a prompt, not an assigned person. For example,
the sponsor may be shown as the suggested owner when a sponsor is
already named. If there is no sponsor, the application says that the
sponsor still needs to be identified.

### Ownership gaps

I check three areas:

- Approvers and consulted stakeholders
- Operational owner after launch
- Delivery capacity and committed people

If the corresponding answer is empty or Unknown, I add an ownership item
with a status, explanation, and follow-up suggestion.

I do not assume that an owner does not exist. I only know that the
intake has not identified one.

### Dependencies to validate

I check the dependency field.

If it is empty or Unknown, I add a prompt to confirm whether
dependencies exist. If the requester has provided dependencies, I
include the answer as requester-provided and recommend validating each
dependency with the team that owns it.

I also check whether both affected systems and integrations are missing.
If both are missing, I add a technical touchpoint prompt asking
engineering to identify systems, interfaces, data flows, and technical
owners.

### Security and privacy follow-up

If the security-review answer is empty, Unknown, or Not needed, I add a
next step to confirm the review approach.

I create a specific security decision and stakeholder question when that
review status is combined with Confidential or Restricted data
classification, or a Yes answer for regulated data.

This is a prompt for a qualified security or privacy review. The
application does not make the compliance decision itself.

### Risk discussion

If risks have been entered, I ask who owns each risk and what evidence
or action will reduce its likelihood or impact.

If no risks are recorded, I generate a general question about what could
prevent delivery or adoption and who would own the response.

### Recommended next steps

I build the next-step list from the assessment and the decision-support
findings. It can include resolving blockers, assigning owners and target
dates to gaps, confirming business and operational ownership, validating
dependencies, and confirming security or privacy reviews.

If none of those conditions apply, I recommend bringing the relevant
stakeholders together to validate assumptions, dependencies, capacity,
and the next decision.

## 9. How I build the report

I use `buildModel()` as the central place to prepare the information for
the report.

It calls the assessment and decision-support functions, groups the
original intake answers, prepares the executive summary, identifies
fields explicitly marked Unknown, and collects the decision items.

I use the same model for the Assessment page and Report & Downloads
page. This keeps the calculations consistent between the two views.

The executive summary is assembled from the information provided for the
problem, affected users, desired outcome, and success measures. If those
details are missing, the report says that there is not enough business
context for a useful summary.

I use `toMarkdown()` to create the report content. This function builds
the report section by section and returns one Markdown string.

The Markdown is also the common source for the copy action, Markdown
download, and Word document generation. This avoids maintaining separate
report-writing logic for each export format.

## 10. How the download options work

### Copy report

The `copyText()` function first tries the browser Clipboard API through
`navigator.clipboard.writeText()`.

If that fails, it creates a temporary textarea, selects the report text,
and tries the older `document.execCommand("copy")` method.

### Markdown download

The `download()` function creates a `Blob` from the Markdown report,
creates a temporary object URL, and clicks a temporary download link.

The filename is based on the request title. I remove characters that are
not suitable for a simple filename and use `request` if the title is
empty.

### Word document download

I wanted the Word export to work without sending intake information to a
document service or relying on a server.

For that reason, I generate the `.docx` file in the browser.

The process is:

1.  I generate the report as Markdown using `toMarkdown()`.
2.  `markdownToWordXml()` converts the Markdown lines into
    WordprocessingML paragraphs.
3.  I map report headings to Word styles such as Title, Heading 1, and
    Heading 2.
4.  I preserve bold text markers and escape XML characters so that
    user-entered text does not break the document XML.
5.  I create the Word document package files: content types,
    relationships, document XML, and styles XML.
6.  `makeStoredZip()` packages those files into a ZIP structure. A DOCX
    file is a ZIP package containing the document XML and related files.
7.  `downloadDocx()` creates a Blob with the DOCX MIME type and starts
    the download.

I wrote the ZIP packaging code directly in JavaScript. It uses a CRC32
table and writes the ZIP local headers, central directory, and end
record. I used the stored ZIP method, so the file entries are not
compressed.

The Word output supports the headings, paragraphs, lists, quote text,
and bold labels used by my report. It is intended to be an editable
document that can be opened in Microsoft Word or Google Docs.

### Print

The Print report action calls the browser’s `window.print()` function. I
added print-specific CSS to hide the navigation, action buttons,
toolbar, footer, and review links so the report is easier to print or
save as PDF.

## 11. How I handled the layout

I used CSS variables for the main colors, text colors, borders, and
status colors. This makes it easier to keep the pages visually
consistent.

I used a two-column layout on wider screens, with navigation on the left
and the active page on the right. On smaller screens, the layout changes
to one column and the navigation can be opened with the **Jump to a
step** button.

I also added:

- Responsive report grids
- Visible keyboard focus styles
- `aria-live` areas for progress and validation messages
- A focus move to the main content after navigation
- A reduced-motion rule
- A print stylesheet

The colors also respond to the browser’s light or dark color-scheme
preference.

## 12. What I deliberately did not build

I made a few decisions to keep the first version focused.

I did not add a backend, user accounts, a database, shared multi-user
editing, or external APIs. I also did not add an AI model or automated
portfolio scoring.

The application works with one intake at a time and saves it in the
current browser. It does not compare several initiatives or decide which
one should be funded or prioritized.

I also do not treat a missing answer as proof that a team has failed to
do something. For example, a missing operational owner means I need to
confirm the ownership, not that no operational team exists.

The assessment and decision-support rules are prompts based on the
information entered. They cannot verify whether that information is
correct or replace the judgment of the sponsor, TPM, engineering,
security, privacy, finance, or operational teams.

## 13. Data handling and limitations

I store intake answers in browser `localStorage` under the key
`tpm-intake-v1`.

This means the answers remain in the same browser profile and on the
same site origin. They are not shared between different browsers or
devices. Clearing browser site data can remove them.

The application code does not send the answers to a server or external
API. However, because the app is hosted as a public GitHub Pages site, I
would not enter confidential, personal, regulated, or company-sensitive
information unless the organization has approved that environment and
use.

The pattern checks are deliberately simple. They can flag an answer that
is acceptable in context or miss a problem that requires human judgment.
The date check also depends on the browser being able to parse the
entered date.

## 14. How I would test changes

When I change the application, I want to check both the page behavior
and the generated outputs.

My basic manual test would include:

1.  Open the application with no saved intake and confirm that the first
    page loads.
2.  Enter answers, refresh the browser, and confirm that the answers and
    current page are restored.
3.  Mark a field as Unknown and confirm that the owner field appears.
4.  Enter vague wording, a short answer, or a solution-first problem
    statement and confirm that the relevant message appears.
5.  Set a hard deadline without a date driver and confirm that the
    cross-field warning appears.
6.  Enter Confidential or Restricted data, or mark regulated data as
    Yes, and confirm the security-review prompt.
7.  Leave the problem or sponsor empty or Unknown and confirm that the
    assessment becomes Not actionable.
8.  Resolve the blockers but leave another required field incomplete and
    confirm that the status becomes Needs clarification.
9.  Complete the required information and review whether the status
    becomes Ready for Triage.
10. Check the Assessment page and confirm that Review answer takes me
    back to the correct intake section.
11. Check the decision-support sections with missing and provided
    answers.
12. Generate the report and compare the browser version, copied version,
    Markdown file, and DOCX file.
13. Open the DOCX in Word or Google Docs and check headings, lists, and
    the report content.
14. Use the print action and confirm that the navigation and buttons are
    hidden.
15. Use Start over and confirm that the saved intake is cleared.

I also check the JavaScript syntax after making code changes. That
catches syntax errors, but it does not replace testing the application
in a real browser.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Final note

I built the TPM Intake Assistant to make project intake more useful for
the people involved. The technical approach reflects that goal: keep the
application simple, make the rules visible, keep the answers close to
the user, and produce a report that can support a real discussion.

I expect to keep improving the questions and rules as I use the
application and learn where the intake process can be clearer.
